# Phase 1 — Make the actual game fun

This is the next thing to build.

Do **not** touch RL, LTX, public submissions, or elaborate lore yet.

## 1.1 Build 10–12 policy archetypes

Examples:

```text
CENTER
holds central control

RUSH
maximizes early contact

ORBIT
maintains tangential separation

COUNTER
punishes closing velocity

EDGE-BAIT
deliberately approaches rim to lure chase

SPIN-SAVER
minimizes actuator cost

LATE-BURST
conserves energy until opponent weakens

PRESSURE
constant controlled engagement

FEINT
switches behavior based on opponent response

ADAPTIVE
uses prior-round observations

PROBER
uses early rounds for information

MIXED
stochastic-looking but seed-deterministic policy
```

Every policy should be simple enough to inspect.

## 1.2 Build body archetypes

Start with maybe 8.

```text
HEAVY
high mass
high inertia
low control

LIGHT
low mass
high control
lower collision stability

WIDE
larger radius
more contact leverage

COMPACT
smaller target
less leverage

HIGH-SPIN
strong endurance
less acceleration

GRIPPY
high contact friction

SLIPPERY
low friction

BALANCED
baseline
```

No exotic abilities yet.

## 1.3 Run the matrix

With:

```text
12 policies
×
8 bodies
×
several seeds
```

you already have a serious experiment space.

Build:

```text
scripts/run_matrix.py
```

Output:

```text
body
policy
opponent_body
opponent_policy
seed
winner
reason
duration
collisions
spin remaining
energy remaining
```

Use DuckDB or Parquet once this gets large.

## 1.4 Diagnose whether the game is actually strategic

Calculate:

```text
win-rate matrix
body advantage
policy advantage
seed sensitivity
match duration
termination reasons
collision count
energy use
center control
```

Most importantly:

```text
does A beat B
does B beat C
does C beat A
```

You want **non-transitivity**.

Bad game:

```text
Policy X beats everything
Body Y beats everything
```

Good game:

```text
Rush beats Spin-Saver
Counter beats Rush
Orbit beats Counter
Pressure beats Orbit
```

### Exit condition

Do not move on until:

* several viable strategies exist;
* body choice matters but does not decide everything;
* strategy can overcome some mechanical disadvantages;
* no universal dominant policy exists;
* matches terminate in a healthy mixture of ring-out/spin-out/burst;
* seeds affect details without making results meaningless.

